{"kind":"request","text":"Replaces 0db97ef4, which shared the dead basis of the supersession cascade. The defect is unchanged.\n\nThe comment at cmd/gs/main.go:136 states a trust-boundary error, and it is where I took the same error into docs/getting-started.md. It reads: \"No default name. The operator seeded here signs the genesis and every grant that follows, so who it is has to be a choice someone made.\"\n\nThe second clause is wrong twice over. app.Init passes sequencerKey to kernel.Create, which hands that key to store.SignedCommit, so the sequencer key signs the genesis commit, not the operator. And \"every grant that follows\" over-scopes what the operator signs: Workspace.BuildActRequest takes the selected actor's private key, so once a second actor is admitted each signs their own acts. Codex found both errors in my documentation, in two separate review rounds; the comment still carries them.\n\nThe comment's conclusion is sound and the behaviour it defends is correct: --operator has no default, and that is right. Only the stated reason is false.\n\nFiled separately from the documentation branch, which is markdown-only.","body":{"conditions":"The comment at cmd/gs/main.go initCommand no longer claims the operator signs the genesis, and no longer claims the operator signs every grant that follows; it names the sequencer key as the genesis signer and scopes the operator's signing to the operator's own acts, while keeping the existing and correct rationale for why --operator has no default; the flag behaviour is unchanged; `go vet` and `make test` pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}